{% section id="delivery" title="Delivery order" %}
Work the four stable panels first (Daily Briefing, Work Threads, Signals, Scratchpad) — each lands independently as a documented, referencable example on the Heddle `/design-system` page plus a preview-template registration. The two WIP panels (Content Explorer, Updated Dashboard) each open with a coordination checkpoint that locks which implementation version becomes the reference; their pattern work does not start until that decision is recorded in this plan's log.
{% /section %}

{% section id="status" title="Status" %}
**draft — not execution-ready.** Two phases are gated on human decisions: P5 needs Katie to confirm develop's merged Content Overview is the browser version (the nod-978 branch appears superseded), and P6 needs Aaron + Katie to agree which Updates Dashboard board goes to production (develop's merged board vs PR 496's theme-following board, with `codex_updates_dashboard_verification` in flight on the adjacent Working Threads surface). The Panel visual survey section shows every candidate side by side. P1–P4 are executable now.
{% /section %}

{% section id="goal" title="Goal" %}
Make six Heddle workspace panels — Daily Briefing, Work Threads, Signals, Scratchpad, Content Explorer (WIP), and Updated Dashboard (WIP) — available as referencable examples in the design system: each with a documented composition pattern (primitives, tokens, layout, states) on the `/design-system` page, a registered preview template in `panel-manifest.json`, and any genuinely shared primitives extracted to Weft. An agent or engineer building a new panel should be able to point at any of these six and copy its pattern without reading the panel's application code.
{% /section %}

{% section id="non-goals" title="Non-goals" %}
- Redesigning or refactoring the panels themselves — this plan documents and templates what exists (or what the decided WIP version ships), it does not change panel behavior.
- Finishing the Content Explorer or Updated Dashboard features. Their in-flight product work stays on their own branches/plans; this plan only consumes the decided version.
- Migrating `--hud-*` transitional aliases to `--weft-*` wholesale — token conformance work stays on the Weft conformance backlog (`weftConformanceBacklog.ts`) except where a pulled pattern would otherwise document raw colors.
- Building new panel-builder block types. Existing block vocabulary in `panel-manifest.json` is assumed sufficient; gaps get logged, not built here.
{% /section %}

{% section id="decision-attention" title="Decision Attention / Low-confidence Areas" %}
**Two product decisions are required before this plan is execution-ready:**

1. **Content Explorer (P5):** confirm that develop's merged `CcoreContentPanel` ("Content Overview") is the version that goes into the browser — branch `katie/nod-978-ccore-content-overview-explorer` appears superseded (PR 242 closed; work merged via PR 244). Owner: **Katie**. See the visual survey below.
2. **Updated Dashboard (P6):** which board version is production-bound — develop's merged Updates Dashboard board or PR 496's theme-following board (`claude/updates-dashboard-board-surface`, NOD-1254) — and how the `codex_updates_dashboard_verification` Working Threads identity work folds in. (`katie/nod-1051-space-dashboard` merged as Settings → Spaces, a different surface.) The Doct doc "Updates Dashboard Related Content Widget Plan" bears on the final shape. Owners: **Aaron + Katie**. See the visual survey below.

**Lower-confidence area:** the exact form each "referencable example" takes (live preview vs static doc section vs `.heddle-panel` example package) is locked per-panel in the phase work below, but reviewers should sanity-check the P1 choice on the first landed panel and adjust the remaining phases if the shape feels wrong.

**UI impact: yes** — new sections and live previews on `/design-system`, but no end-user workspace behavior changes.
{% /section %}

{% section id="why" title="Why this plan exists" %}
The design system's Panel Reference on `/design-system` stops at the original panel set (session context/recap, notes, transcription, knowledge search) — the newer panels (daily-brief, work-board, signals, ccore-content, decision-review) have no reference entries. `panel-manifest.json` registers exactly one preview template (`session-recap`). Meanwhile the panel-builder, panel-authoring docs, and external panel authors all point at the design system as the source of composition truth. The gap means every new panel is designed from scratch or by reading application code, and the WIP panels risk shipping without their patterns ever being captured. Pulling these six patterns closes the loop: the panels people actually build against become the documented examples.
{% /section %}

{% section id="authority-inputs" title="Authority and inputs" %}
- User direction (Katie, 2026-07-29): build panel templates in the design system as referencable examples; Scratchpad "should be good"; Content Explorer version to be coordinated with Katie; Updated Dashboard version to be coordinated between Aaron and Katie.
- Repo boundary: `weft/AGENTS.md` — panel-manifest, panel authoring docs, `/design-system` gallery pages, and panel surfaces belong to **Heddle**; shared primitives, tokens, and the manifest lockstep gates belong to **Weft**. A pattern only moves to Weft when two products could want it.
- Existing pattern docs to match: `NotesCardPatternDoc`, `SignalsErrorStatesSection`, `UndocumentedPatternsLivePreview` in `heddle/src/design-system/` and `DesignSystemPage.tsx`.
- Panel implementations: `daily-brief/DailyBriefPanel.tsx` (+ `dailyBrief.css`, `signalPreferences.ts`, `sources.ts`), `work-board/WorkBoardRoute.tsx` (`WorkBoardHUDPanel`, + `viewModel.ts`, `workBoard.css`), signals composites `components/panels/signals/*` + `panel-builder/SignalBlockRuntime.tsx` + `signalPanelConfig.ts`, `components/panels/NotesPanel.tsx` + `NoteCard.tsx`, `ccore-content/CcoreContentPanel.tsx`.
- Related Doct plans: "Working Threads Panel Rework (NOD-1299)", "Scratchpad Panel Persists Notes to CCore (NOD-1287)", "NOD-1315 — Remove Daily Brief Preference Settings", "Updates Dashboard Related Content Widget Plan", "weft design system consolidation".
- Brand rules: Heddle brand package — token-only colors (`--weft-*` / `--hud-*` semantics), sentence case, WCAG 2.1 AA invariants (focus-visible, reduced motion, touch targets).
{% /section %}

{% section id="current-reality" title="Current implementation reality" %}
- `PANEL_REGISTRY` (`heddle/src/app/mode/panelRegistry.ts`) defines 12 built-in panels; the six in scope map to ids `daily-brief` ("Daily Brief"), `work-board` ("Working Threads"), `signals` ("Signals"), `notes` ("Notes" — the Scratchpad home), `ccore-content` ("Content Overview" — the Content Explorer), and the Updated Dashboard which is **not yet a registry panel** — it lives on branches as a board/space-dashboard surface.
- `DesignSystemPage.tsx` (~3,800 lines) renders `PANELS_REF` entries only for the original panel set; none of the six in-scope panels except Notes has a reference entry. Product-pattern deep dives exist for Notes cards (`NotesCardPatternDoc` + live preview via `UndocumentedPatternsLivePreview`, including the scratchpad variant) and Signals error states (`SignalsErrorStatesSection`).
- `panel-manifest.json` → `panelBuilder.previewTemplates` is `["session-recap"]`. The separate `panel-builder/panelTemplates.ts` ships seven builder drafts (daily-summary, meeting-recap, project-status, signal-dashboard, intake-form, checklist, prompt-runner) — these are builder seed drafts, not documented reference patterns, and none is wired to the design-system page.
- `docs/panel-authoring/examples/` contains referencable panel-package examples (`status-dashboard.heddle-panel`, `dense-list-detail.heddle-panel`, `action-planner.heddle-panel`, plus `.example.tsx` built-ins) — a second registration surface the six patterns should slot into where the panel is package-representable.
- Signals rendering is already componentized: `SignalRow`, `SignalFilterBar`, `SignalPriorityPill`, `SignalActorBadge`, `SignalSourceIcon`, `priorityColor.ts` under `components/panels/signals/`, consumed by both the built-in panel (via `SignalBlockRuntime`) and custom signal blocks. Weft already ships `signal-filter-chip-group` and `signal-group-collapsible` primitives.
- Daily Brief has its own CSS file (`dailyBrief.css`) and source/preference modules — the most "app-shaped" of the four stable panels, and the likeliest to surface raw-color or unprefixed-token cleanups when pulled.
- Work Threads: `WorkBoardRoute.tsx` is 1,673 lines exporting both a route and `WorkBoardHUDPanel`; NOD-1299 reworked this panel and its Doct plan documents the intended composition.
- WIP branches on the heddle repo: `katie/nod-978-ccore-content-overview-explorer` (Content Explorer), `claude/updates-dashboard-board-surface` and `katie/nod-1051-space-dashboard` (dashboard candidates), `codex_updates_dashboard_verification` (currently checked out in the main heddle working copy).
- Weft gates that constrain any primitive extraction: manifest lockstep (`npm run verify`), prop-contract snapshot (`npm run props`), token purity (`check-pure-token-file.mjs`, `check-raw-colors.mjs`), a11y suites in `npm test`.
{% /section %}

{% section id="product-intent" title="Product intent alignment" %}
- Advances the design system's core promise: the documented pattern and the shipped panel are the same artifact, so panel authors (human or agent) copy working patterns instead of inventing near-misses.
- Respects the Weft/Heddle boundary — patterns document in Heddle where they live; only genuinely shared primitives graduate to Weft, keeping the package consumable by more than one product.
- Protects the WIP surfaces from premature canonization: a pattern only becomes "the example" after the owning humans pick the version, so the design system never teaches a rejected iteration.
- Keeps accessibility and token discipline shipped-by-default: every pulled pattern passes the same scanner and a11y gates as new Weft components, so referencing an example can't propagate raw colors or focus regressions.
{% /section %}

{% section id="locked-decisions" title="Locked decisions" %}
{% decision id="decision-1" %}**Referencable example = three artifacts per panel.** (1) A `PANELS_REF` entry (or equivalent) in the Panel Reference section; (2) a product-pattern doc section with a live or static composition preview, matching the existing `NotesCardPatternDoc` / `SignalsErrorStatesSection` shape; (3) a preview-template registration in `panel-manifest.json` `panelBuilder.previewTemplates` so the builder and external authors can enumerate it.{% /decision %}
{% decision id="decision-2" %}**Patterns land in Heddle, primitives land in Weft.** Doc sections, manifest entries, and previews go in the Heddle repo per the AGENTS.md boundary. A component is extracted to `weft/src/ui/` only if the pattern review shows a second product would consume it; extraction follows Weft's full 10-point component checklist and gates.{% /decision %}
{% decision id="decision-3" %}**WIP panels gate on recorded decisions, not merged code.** P5 and P6 start with a decision checkpoint logged in this plan (version chosen, owner, date). The pattern is pulled from the chosen version even if that branch has not merged, but the design-system entry ships flagged "under construction" until the version is in `main`.{% /decision %}
{% decision id="decision-4" %}**Scratchpad is a verification pass, not a rebuild.** Notes/scratchpad documentation already exists (NotesCardPatternDoc, live preview, NOD-1287 CCore persistence). P4 verifies it against the current implementation, closes drift, and adds the missing preview-template registration — nothing more.{% /decision %}
{% decision id="decision-5" %}**Token cleanliness is enforced on the pattern, not the whole panel.** If pulling a pattern exposes raw colors or unprefixed tokens in the example markup, the example is written token-clean and the underlying file is ticketed on the Weft conformance backlog rather than block this plan (except where the fix is trivial and local).{% /decision %}
{% /section %}

{% section id="acceptance" title="Acceptance Criteria" %}
{% acceptance id="ac-1" %}**AC1 — Reference coverage:** Each of the six panels has a Panel Reference entry and a product-pattern doc section on `/design-system` naming its primitives, tokens, layout regions, and states (loading, empty, error where applicable).{% /acceptance %}
{% acceptance id="ac-2" %}**AC2 — Template registration:** `panel-manifest.json` `previewTemplates` enumerates a template id for each landed panel pattern, and the design-system Preview templates card renders them; ids resolve wherever preview templates are consumed.{% /acceptance %}
{% acceptance id="ac-3" %}**AC3 — Token and a11y cleanliness:** Every example's markup uses only `var(--weft-*)` / sanctioned `--hud-*` semantic tokens (raw-color scanner clean) and preserves focus-visible, reduced-motion, and touch-target invariants.{% /acceptance %}
{% acceptance id="ac-4" %}**AC4 — Gates green:** Heddle's design-system/panel test suites pass; if any primitive is extracted to Weft, `npm run verify`, `npm run props`, `npm test`, and the token/raw-color checks pass there with manifest + snapshot updated in lockstep.{% /acceptance %}
{% acceptance id="ac-5" %}**AC5 — WIP decisions recorded:** The Content Explorer and Updated Dashboard entries each cite the decided version (branch/commit, decider, date) in this plan's log, and their design-system entries carry an "under construction" flag until that version merges to main.{% /acceptance %}
{% /section %}

{% section id="bdd" title="BDD Scenarios" %}
{% bdd id="bdd-ac1-1" title="BDD-AC1-1 — Panel author references a pattern" %}
**Given** the `/design-system` page is open, **when** an author navigates to the Panel Reference or Product patterns section for any of the six panels, **then** they can read the panel's primitives, tokens, and layout regions and see a rendered composition example, **and** they never need to open the panel's application source to reproduce the pattern.
{% /bdd %}
{% bdd id="bdd-ac2-1" title="BDD-AC2-1 — Preview templates enumerate" %}
**Given** `panel-manifest.json` is loaded by the design-system page, **when** the Preview templates card renders, **then** it lists a template id for every landed panel pattern alongside `session-recap`.
{% /bdd %}
{% bdd id="bdd-ac3-1" title="BDD-AC3-1 — Examples are token-clean" %}
**Given** the raw-color pattern from `weft/tooling/raw-color-pattern.js`, **when** it runs over each new example/doc-section file, **then** it reports no raw hex/rgb/hsl outside sanctioned exceptions.
{% /bdd %}
{% bdd id="bdd-ac5-1" title="BDD-AC5-1 — WIP panel gated on decision" %}
**Given** the Content Explorer or Updated Dashboard phase is starting, **when** no version decision is recorded in the Decisions / Deviations Log, **then** the phase does not proceed past its checkpoint step, **and** the design system remains unchanged for that panel.
{% /bdd %}
{% /section %}

